# Connect on boot, if this machine has been told to.
#
#   floppa autostart on
#
# The setting is *not* whether this unit is enabled; the package enables it and it stays enabled,
# doing nothing until asked. That is not the shape anyone would choose — `systemctl is-enabled`
# would be the obvious place to look — and the reason is a limit in systemd: it hands polkit the
# unit's name for `manage-units` (start, stop) but not for `manage-unit-files` (enable, disable),
# verified against polkit 127. So no rule can let the `floppa` group enable *this one* unit without
# a password prompt; only one that lets it enable any unit at all, which is root by another name.
#
# Group membership is the authority every other thing a client does here is gated by, and
# connecting on boot belongs inside it. So the preference lives where that authority reaches — the
# service's own state directory — and `--if-enabled` asks for it.
#
# A unit of its own rather than something the tunnel service does when it starts, because the
# tunnel service is also started by its socket — every `floppa status` would otherwise reconnect a
# VPN somebody had deliberately turned off. Being *asked* is different from being *started*, and
# only a caller can tell the two apart. So this is a caller: one shot, which connects to the socket
# (activating the service if it is not up) and asks it for whatever it last had.
#
# Which tunnel that is, is not stated here. The service wrote it down the last time one connected,
# beside the configs, in a directory only root can read — so the request is "whatever you had" and
# the process that knows answers it. A machine that has never connected is told so and nothing
# happens.

[Unit]
Description=Connect the Floppa VPN tunnel on boot
Documentation=https://github.com/okhsunrog/floppa-vpn
Requires=floppa-vpn.socket
After=floppa-vpn.socket network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/floppa resume --if-enabled
RemainAfterExit=yes

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
